### How edits move actions

| Edit | What happens to the clip's actions |
|---|---|
| Trim the start | Actions keep their clip time, so they stay put relative to the clip's new start |
| Trim the end | Actions stay put, so one can end up running past the clip's new end; only the part inside the clip plays. You can still move it earlier, shorten it or change its curve, but not make it end any later |
| Slip or Roll | Actions stay where they are in clip time |
| Change the speed, or rate-stretch | Actions keep their place as a fraction of the clip |
| Split | Actions after the cut go to the second half; one that spans the cut is divided between the halves |
| Add a transition at a hard cut | The incoming clip's actions move with its new first frame, so a zoom stays on the frame it was drawn over |
| Duplicate, Copy and Paste | The copies keep their actions |
| Nest | The clips keep their actions inside the nest |
| Detach Audio | The new audio clip gets a copy of every action, so volume actions keep working |
| Freeze Frame | The still has no actions; the look at the frozen frame is baked into its own properties |
| Add Blurred Background | The backdrop copy has no actions, so a zoom on the foreground doesn't zoom the backdrop |
| Paste Attributes | Actions are not copied |

## Motion presets

A motion preset says what a clip **does** rather than where it is. Each clip has three slots: an **In** (an entrance at its start), an **Out** (an exit at its end) and a **During** (a movement for its whole length). A preset is tied to the clip's ends, so it moves with a trim. It works on any clip that draws a picture: video, still images, text, shapes, graphics and nests.
| Row | What it does |
|---|---|
| In | The graphic frame the clip's first frame shows (read only) |
| Length | How long the graphic's own timeline runs. Typing a value pins it |
| Follow Clip | On (the default): the graphic's timeline ends when the clip ends |

Trimming the start of the clip, rolling the cut before it, or splitting it
moves **In**, so the second half of a split carries on where the first left
off rather than starting the animation over. Trimming the end leaves a pinned
Length alone; with Follow Clip on, the graphic's timeline ends at the clip's
new end. A split pins both halves' Length to the original's, so an outro still
plays at the same point. With Follow Clip on, a front trim doesn't shorten the
graphic's timeline either.

When Length is pinned shorter than the clip, frames keep counting past it but
the module's progress stays at its end. Whether the picture then holds or
keeps moving depends on the module: an animation driven by progress holds its
end state, and one driven by frame numbers keeps going.

*Agents:* `set_graphic` (`clips` and a `patch` of `name`, `width`, `height`,
`length` and `props`; `length: null` is Follow Clip; a `null` prop clears it,
except one an action on the clip animates, which is set to the module's
default instead).

/// `transition_in` names the kind, and `start`/`duration` are the span in
/// PROJECT time that both clips cover.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub struct Overlap {
    pub earlier: u32,
    pub later: u32,
    pub start: Ticks,
    pub duration: Ticks,
}

/// The maximum overlap permitted for this pair by the half-shorter rule.
pub(crate) fn overlap_limit(a: &Clip, b: &Clip) -> Ticks {
    a.duration.min(b.duration) / 2
}

/// The overlap between two clips, `earlier` being the one that starts
/// first. `None` when they merely touch: a zero-length overlap is not a
/// transition, it is a cut, and treating it as one would put a degenerate
/// division into every progress computation downstream.
fn overlap_of(earlier: &Clip, later: &Clip) -> Option<Overlap> {
    let start = earlier.start.max(later.start);
    let end = earlier
        .start
        .saturating_add(earlier.duration)
        .min(later.start.saturating_add(later.duration));
    let duration = end.saturating_sub(start);
    if duration <= 0 {
        return None;
    }
    Some(Overlap {
        earlier: earlier.id,
        later: later.id,
        start,
        duration,
    })
}
}

/// Gives every clip that now overlaps its predecessor and has no
/// `transition_in` a cross dissolve. An overlap IS a transition (spec
/// §5.2), so a clip that gains one and would otherwise render as two
/// stacked layers gets one rather than nothing.
///
/// It is MINTED (spec §11, R5-2) — eased and crossfading — and not
/// `Transition::default()`, which is the FILE default a version-6 project's
/// overlap reads as. A clip that gains an overlap now is a new transition
/// now.
pub(crate) fn set_gained_transitions(project: &mut Project, tracks: &[u32]) {
    let mut gained: Vec<u32> = Vec::new();
    for track in tracks {
        for clip in project.clips_on_track(*track) {
            if clip.transition_in.is_none() && project.overlap_before(clip.id).is_some() {
                gained.push(clip.id);
            }
        }
    }
    for id in gained {
        if let Some(clip) = project.clip_mut(id) {
            clip.transition_in = Some(Transition::minted(TransitionKind::default()));
        }
    }
}

/// THE too-short sentence, for both forms of `add_transition` (spec §11
/// #88): a request under one frame of the canvas's grid, naming `one`, the
/// smallest duration the form would take.
pub(crate) fn transition_too_short(clip: u32, duration: Ticks, one: Ticks) -> String {
    format!(
        "a transition of {duration} ticks is too short to make at the cut before clip \
         {clip}; pass a duration of at least {one} ticks"
    )
}

/// The pair as [`Project::add_transition`] would leave it: `earlier` grown
/// by `tail` ticks at its end, `later` moved back `head` ticks and grown by
/// the same, its `in` following — a graphic's own `in` too, through
/// `graphic_advanced` (spec §11 G-18·a).
///
/// One function, so the trial the overlap rule is checked against and the
/// clips that are finally written cannot be two different arithmetics.
/// Actions and the transition itself are the caller's, because the trial
/// does not care about either.
fn extended(earlier: &Clip, later: &Clip, tail: Ticks, head: Ticks) -> (Clip, Clip) {
    let mut grown = earlier.clone();
    grown.duration = grown.duration.saturating_add(tail);

    let mut moved = later.clone();
    // The source ticks that lie under `head` ticks of timeline at this
    // clip's speed, subtracted — `source_window.rs`'s one mover run
    // BACKWARD, and the reason the handle above is measured in TIMELINE
    // time and never divided by the speed a second time.
    moved.source = source_advanced(&later.source, head.saturating_neg(), later.speed);
    Err(format!(
        "clip {} is not an adjustment clip and cannot take a qualifier",
        clip.id
    ))
}

/// The overlap rule's sentence for an overlapping pair that holds an
/// adjustment clip, naming the later one when both are; `None` when neither
/// is. Its one caller is `check_overlap_rule`, with a pair it has already
